%% 10 — Two app servers in production: what scales and what does not ([5.3] #19)
%% Drawn from: ADR 0011, ADR 0020, ADR 0021, ADR 0026 (and its connection-pool
%%   amendment), ADR 0030, docs/deployment.md "Connection budget" and "Scaling",
%%   deploy/production/{compose.yml,release.sh,migrate.sh}, infra/terraform/{variables,load_balancer,databases}.tf,
%%   backend/ledger_service/core/config.py, backend/ledger_service/main.py,
%%   backend/ledger_service/service/local_copy.py, backend/market_service/service/closing.py,
%%   DECISIONS.md "Each service's connection pool is a setting, sized to the database's ceiling"
%% Pool + overflow per server (compose.yml): auth 3+1, market 3+2, ledger 6+2, audit
%% 1+1 = 19 client connections, onto DigitalOcean's four PgBouncer pools
%% (databases.tf: auth 6, market 6, ledger 24, audit 2 = 38 of about 47 on the
%% 2 GB plan), however many servers run. The migrate steps connect directly, one
%% at a time (migrate.sh), in the room the pools leave.
%% LEADERBOARD_REFRESH_ENABLED is true on the first server only (release.sh app_env_for).
%% Both local-copy refreshers run on every ledger, about every second (ADR 0030,
%% main.py _start_copy_refreshers); a trade calls nobody. They, and the reads and
%% marks of settle and void, reach auth and market on the ledger's own server:
%% compose sets no MARKET_SERVICE_URL or AUTH_SERVICE_URL, so config.py's defaults
%% name those containers.
%% The local run of the same idea, one host and one nginx, is docs/horizontal-scaling.md.
%% ELK lays siblings out in reverse source order here, so app-2 is written first.
